A1. What the party may do on account of the 18th amendment. ~ SPLIT
A6. For this fellow's sake, we hope things will pick up. ~ PETES
A11. "When words fail yuh." ~ IRATE
A12. As far as we know this is still conscious. ~ AWARE
A13. A prominent figure in sport circles. ~ NUMERAL
A16. If this has gone too far, it is all rot. ~ RIPENED
A18. This always remains the same. ~ IDEM
A19. This man is always inquiring about some body. ~ CORONER
A21. A tonic for singers. ~ LA
A22. This will make some change in China. (plur.) ~ SENS
A23. Unexpected unwelcome guests. ~ RAIDERS
A24. Where the family keep their old shoes now. ~ ON
A25. This has a bombastic way with it. ~ TNT
A26. No matter what your problems are, you can always count on these. ~ FINGERS
A27. The bunch in power. ~ INS
A28. You will have to take a Frenchman's word for this. ~ ET
A29. What a vacuum cleaner will do to a house of dust. ~ RID
A30. It would be easy to make a pun out of this. ~ MOT
A31. If you wish to hurry up matters, make this work. ~ ERG
A32. This is plain on the face of it. ~ MUG
A33. In some states every motorist is compelled to stop for this. ~ GAS
A34. This has left the city and will be put to work on the farm. ~ HOE
A36. A squealer. ~ PIG
A37. Beyond the horizon. ~ FAR
A38. We hope this will start a move in the right direction. ~ VAN
A39. Letters of credit. ~ CR
A40. A Wrigley product. ~ EEL
A41. This can even "high hat" the Prince of Wales. ~ CORONET
A43. A dummy John. ~ DOE
A44. This is just 21. (Hor.) ~ LA
A45. A pressing affair. ~ MANGLER
A46. This will also make some change in Japan. (plur.) ~ SENS
A47. This aesthetic miss is a knock-out. ~ AN
A48. We will try and make this simple for you. ~ ARTLESS
A49. A former big executive. ~ TAFT
A50. What brother punched sister on. ~ PURPOSE
A52. The kind of woman that gets her own way. ~ TEARFUL
A54. Stick in the mud. ~ STALL
A55. Followers of Mother Goose ~ GEESE
A56. Former name of Rocky Mt. National Park. ~ ESTES
A57. "Whatever possessed it." ~ OWNER

D1. There is something ominous about this. ~ SINISTER
D2. But here is a sensible kind of idea. ~ PRUDENT
D3. For crying out loud, you will gain nothing from this. ~ LAMENT
D4. We will give you all the details for this. ~ ITEMS
D5. This one labels pa as pater. ~ TER
D6. They often serve these just for the sake of appearances. ~ PAPERS
D7. Ever since baseball first started these pitchers have been open mouthed. ~ EWERS
D8. A coat of this will be quite the mode this summer. ~ TAN
D9. This will come to you shortly. ~ ERELONG
D10. He bought one of these at a bargain and at his wife's suggestion turned it into a hot-dog stand, and what a hit he made. ~ SEDANS
D14. This may be distasteful to you. ~ ACRID
D15. Something that always creates interest. ~ LOAN
D16. Horse play. (plur.) ~ RODEOS
D17. That tired feeling. ~ INERT
D20. The role of the average senator. ~ RIGMAROLE
D26. One of the Newtons. ~ FIG
D27. "Some call it madness." ~ IRE
D29. When the maestro was only 18 months old he learned to play on this. ~ RUG
D31. It took ages to make this. ~ EON
D32. One-thousandth of an inch and three-fourths of a mile. ~ MIL
D33. Whatever you hear, do not swallow this. ~ GARGLE
D34. No matter how much this is blocked, it will continue to go on ahead. ~ HAT
D35. His chief ambition is to throw a big party. ~ WRESTLER
D36. These are "nuts" about the circus. ~ PEANUTS
D37. The type that is always getting into print. (plur.) ~ FONTS
D38. This has rhythmic feet. ~ VERSE
D39. Ball up. ~ CONFUSE
D40. Only time can do this. ~ ELAPSE
D41. Joyful twitters. ~ CAROLS
D42. A hang-out for the St. Louis cardinals. ~ NEST
D43. What a big boom might do. ~ DEAFEN
D45. Some sap. ~ MAPLE
D46. What the family can do with the Sunday paper. ~ STREW
D51. This reminds one of the "Catskills." ~ RAT
D53. There is no future for this. ~ AGO


